TEST 11346 -- PIM-DM End-to-end test
===========================================================================
Run:    2026-09-30 18:27-19:12 NZDT, tb470, bench-runner (queue CAMPAIGN-QUEUE-2026-09-29.md
        row 12). Hand-driven: no framework script. That session ended at ~19:12 before it
        wrote the log or restored. On Terrence's 2026-10-01 instruction ("write it up from the
        2026-09-30 evidence, then restore -- no re-run") this log was written on 2026-10-01
        from the committed evidence and the tb470 /tmp/ck12 console transcripts. NOTHING WAS
        RE-MEASURED. The restore was done 2026-10-01 08:02-08:1x NZDT (below).
Build:  awplus_main-20260923-20 (stack and IE520-sa)
DUT:    IE520 3-member VCStack "IE520-stk" (members 1/3/4, master member 3), router MAC
        0000.cd37.0d6f (virtual MAC). sw1 = the standalone IE520-sa running PIM-DM.
Case:   AWPTCM-T11346 (ck.db), objective "PIM-DM works as expected DUT can route multicast
        traffic using PIM-DM". Precondition (topology): "IXIA -- DUT -- sw1(running PIM-DM)--
        IXIA". One step: "Setup a PIM-DM network" -> expected "Multicast client able to get
        multicast stream". Here tcpreplay/scapy on tb470 stand in for both Ixia ports.

VERDICT: PASS.
  A two-router PIM-DM network (DUT = first-hop router with the source directly connected,
  sw1 = last-hop router and IGMP querier for the client) formed PIMv2 dense-mode neighbours.
  The client's IGMP join got the multicast stream end to end:
    - 1000 pps, 30 s: 30,001 / 30,001 delivered (100.0000 %).
    - line rate, 1514 B, 30 s: 984.30 Mbps, 2,438,001 / 2,438,001 delivered (100.0000 %,
      0 kernel drops); 81,267 pps = 99.99 % of the 81,274 pps 1G maximum at this size.
  Controls:
    - baseline (IGMP join, no multicast routing): 0 / 10,001 delivered.
    - the frames arrive routed twice: TTL 16 sent -> 14 received, source MAC = the SA's
      vlan3113 MAC.
    - Leave -> prune: the stream stops and the DUT's downstream list empties; re-join ->
      graft: it resumes (2 runs).
  The (S,G) route is in hardware on all three stack members.

TOPOLOGY (standing bench as at 2026-09-30, no recable; all addressing scratch)
  NB: on 2026-09-30 tb470 eth1 was on stack port3.0.13. Since 2026-10-01 (commit 2ab61f7) it is
  on port3.0.10 and port3.0.13 is an empty cage; this log describes the bench the run had.
  source eth1 (tb470) -- stack port3.0.13      vlan3111 10.113.1.1/24   [source host 10.113.1.2, fake MAC]
  stack port4.0.26 (member 4, 1000BASE-SX) -- SA port1.0.26   vlan3112 10.113.2.1/30 <-> .2/30  (PIM-DM)
  SA port1.0.2 -- eth2 (tb470)                 vlan3113 10.113.3.1/24   [receiver 10.113.3.2 = fake
                                                MAC 02:31:13:00:00:03, IGMPv2 from scapy]
  Group 239.113.1.1, UDP 5000 -> 5001, TTL 16 (tools/mkmc.py). SA static route
  10.113.1.0/24 via 10.113.2.1 (RPF toward the source). The SX link's baseline VLAN 4000 and
  the vlan1 membership of port3.0.13 and SA port1.0.2 were changed for the case.

